Chapter 4: Private Conversation

The night had deepened into something quieter—too quiet.
Even the insects seemed to hesitate at the edges of the estate, their usual chorus reduced to thin, uncertain threads. The air itself felt… held. Waiting.
Sukuna noticed.
Of course he did.
He stood at the far end of the outer courtyard, arms folded loosely into his sleeves, gaze tilted toward the tree line where the dark pressed thickest. His presence alone warped the space—heavy, oppressive, like something ancient had chosen to breathe there.
Behind him, faint through the open screens, Tsukihiko laughed.
A soft, bright sound.
It should not have existed in a world like this.
Sukuna’s eyes flickered.
Then stilled again.
—
Alexei did not announce himself this time.
He appeared where the shadows bent strangely near the boundary of the estate, stepping forward with the same easy, almost careless posture—but there was nothing careless about the way his eyes moved.
Measured.
Calculating.
Hungry.
“Still watching the dark,” Alexei said lightly, though his voice didn’t quite reach warmth. “Or has the dark started watching you back?”
Sukuna didn’t turn.
“Speak carefully,” he replied, tone mild—too mild. “You’ve already overstayed your usefulness.”
Alexei huffed a quiet laugh, rolling his shoulders as if easing tension that hadn’t left him in years.
“Usefulness,” he echoed. “That’s one way to describe what I’m doing for you.”
Now Sukuna turned.
Slowly.
And the air… tightened.
There it was—that thing beneath him. That pressure. That quiet, coiled violence that made lesser men choke on their own breath.
Alexei didn’t flinch.
But his smile faded.
“Who comes next,” he said, voice dropping—stripped now of pretense, “won’t talk.”
Silence.
A long one.
Sukuna’s gaze sharpened—not with curiosity.
With interest.
“Explain.”
Alexei exhaled through his nose, glancing once—not toward the trees, not toward the estate—but toward the space in between.
Like he was measuring something unseen.
“You felt it,” he said. “Didn’t you?”
A pause.
“Something touched your barrier.”
Sukuna’s lip curled slightly.
“Yes.”
“Then you already know more than most,” Alexei murmured.
He stepped closer—not enough to challenge, but enough to enter Sukuna’s space deliberately.
“That wasn’t a test,” he continued. “It wasn’t a warning.”
His eyes lifted.
“It was a measurement.”
Sukuna’s expression didn’t change.
But something behind it… shifted.
“Of what?” he asked.
Alexei smiled again—but this time it didn’t reach his eyes.
“Of you.”
—
The wind moved.
Soft.
Cold.
Alexei folded his arms, gaze drifting—not aimlessly, never aimlessly—but like a man laying pieces on a board only he could fully see.
“You think power is what matters,” he said. “That if something threatens you, you crush it. End of story.”
His eyes flicked back.
“You’re not wrong.”
A beat.
“But you’re incomplete.”
Sukuna’s gaze darkened.
“Careful.”
Alexei didn’t stop.
“Because where I come from,” he said, quieter now, “power is just… the bait.”
That landed.
Sukuna’s interest sharpened further.
“Then what hunts?” he asked.
Alexei’s smile returned—thin, sharp.
“Control.”
—
The word lingered.
Heavy.
Ugly.
Real.
Alexei reached up slowly, fingers brushing the collar at his throat—not tugging, not pulling.
Just… touching it.
“Do you know what this does?” he asked.
Sukuna’s eyes dropped to it.
He had seen it.
Felt it.
But never… understood it.
“Explain,” he said again.
Alexei tilted his head slightly.
“It’s not just restraint,” he said. “Not just punishment.”
His fingers pressed lightly against the metal.
“It’s ownership.”
A pause.
“Body,” he added. “Mind.”
Another.
“And voice.”
That… caught something.
Sukuna’s eyes narrowed.
“Voice,” he repeated.
Alexei nodded.
“You’ve seen cursed speech,” he said. “Heard it. Felt what it does.”
Sukuna didn’t respond.
He didn’t need to.
“Yes,” Alexei continued softly, “now imagine that… but permanent.”
Silence.
“The collar doesn’t just force obedience,” he went on. “It rewrites it. Commands don’t feel like commands.”
His gaze sharpened.
“They feel like truth.”
—
For the first time—
Sukuna’s expression changed.
Barely.
But it did.
A flicker.
A calculation.
“Who gives those commands?” he asked.
Alexei’s lips curved slightly.
“You already know the answer to that.”
A beat.
“Not just anyone.”
Another.
“Someone who specializes in it.”
—
The wind stilled again.
And this time—
Even Sukuna felt it.
Not presence.
Not yet.
But… the absence of something.
Like the world was making room.
—
“Binding,” Sukuna said slowly.
Not a question.
A recognition.
Alexei’s smile widened—just a little.
“Yes.”
He shifted his weight, gaze lowering briefly before lifting again.
“There are people,” he said, “who don’t just fight.”
His voice dropped.
“They decide what fights… and what doesn’t.”
A pause.
“And when.”
—
Sukuna stepped closer.
Now there was no space between them.
Only pressure.
Only intent.
“And you’re telling me,” Sukuna said quietly, “someone like that is coming here.”
Alexei held his gaze.
“I’m telling you,” he said, “he’s already decided this ends.”
A beat.
“And you’re not the one who gets to decide how.”
—
That—
That was the first real spark.
It flared behind Sukuna’s eyes—dangerous, immediate.
Possessive.
Violent.
“Everything here,” Sukuna said softly, “is mine.”
Alexei didn’t argue.
He nodded, almost gently.
“I know.”
And then—
He leaned in slightly.
Just enough.
“And that’s exactly why he’s coming.”
—
The silence that followed wasn’t empty.
It was full.
Of implications.
Of inevitability.
Of something tightening its grip around the edges of the world.
—
Sukuna’s mind moved fast.
Too fast.
Threads connecting.
Barrier touched.
No resistance.
No trace.
Measured.
Controlled.
Planned.
Not brute force.
Not chaos.
Something worse.
Precision.
—
“What does he want?” Sukuna asked.
Alexei didn’t hesitate.
“Not what you think.”
A pause.
His gaze flickered—just for a second.
Toward the estate.
Toward the soft glow where Leora was.
Then back.
“He doesn’t want your sons.”
Sukuna’s expression didn’t change.
But the air did.
“Then he’s a fool,” Sukuna said.
Alexei’s smile faded completely now.
“No,” he said quietly.
“He’s not.”
—
Another silence.
He let it sit.
Let it breathe.
Let it grow teeth.
—
Sukuna’s voice dropped further.
“What. Does he want.”
This time—
Alexei took his time.
Just enough.
Just long enough.
To let the answer land before it was spoken.
“To take back what the tribes believe never should have left.”
His eyes held Sukuna’s.
“Her.”
—
The word didn’t echo.
It didn’t need to.
It detonated.
—
Sukuna’s cursed energy surged—sharp, violent, immediate.
The ground beneath them cracked.
The air screamed.
And for a moment—
For just a moment—
Alexei saw it.
The thing Sukuna was when pushed.
Not a man.
Not even a curse.
Something older.
Hungrier.
—
Good.
—
Alexei straightened slightly, expression smoothing back into something almost neutral.
“But he won’t kill her,” he added quickly.
Measured.
Careful.
“He won’t hurt the children.”
Another beat.
“He believes he’s saving her.”
—
That twisted something.
Ugly.
Wrong.
Sukuna’s lip curled.
“Saving,” he repeated, like the word itself offended him.
Alexei nodded.
“He thinks you’re the problem.”
—
That did it.
Not the threat.
Not the implication.
That.
—
Sukuna laughed.
Low.
Cold.
Dangerous.
“Then he’ll die thinking that,” he said.
—
Alexei didn’t smile this time.
He just watched him.
Carefully.
Calculating.
—
“Maybe,” he said softly.
A pause.
“Or maybe…”
His eyes flickered again—just once.
Subtle.
Precise.
“…you’ll learn what it feels like when someone decides you don’t get a choice.”
—
The words hung there.
Sharp.
Deliberate.
Placed.
—
And something in Sukuna—
Shifted.
Not doubt.
Never that.
But something else.
Something far more dangerous.
Awareness.
—
Behind them—
Inside—
Leora laughed softly at something Tsukihiko said.
Warm.
Alive.
Real.
—
And for the first time since Alexei arrived—
Sukuna didn’t turn toward the sound.
—
Alexei noticed.
Of course he did.
—
And very quietly—
So quietly it barely existed—
He exhaled.
Satisfied.
—
The first layer had settled.
The board was set.
—
